{"name":"Blue Screen of Death","hex":"#0033bb","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue screen of death","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An error screen displayed by some operating systems, especially Microsoft Windows, after encountering an unrecoverable error which causes the system to shut down."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_screen_of_death"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue screen of death","blocks":[{"text":"The blue screen of death (BSoD) – or blue screen error, blue screen, fatal error, bugcheck, and officially known as a stop error – is a critical error screen displayed in many iterations of Windows, a series of operating systems by Microsoft. It is used to indicate a system crash, in which the operating system reaches a critical condition where it can no longer operate safely. Its name comes from the blue-colored background used predominantly on the error screens found in the majority of Windows releases, which was changed to black starting with Windows 11 version 24H2."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Windows_9X_BSOD.png/330px-Windows_9X_BSOD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_screen_of_death","wikidata":"Q214795"},{"title":"System crash screen","blocks":[{"text":"In computing, a system crash screen, error screen or screen of death is a visual indicator that appears when an operating system, software application, or hardware encounters a severe issue that prevents normal operation. These screens typically serve as a last-resort mechanism to inform users and system administrators of a critical failure. An error screen may display technical information such as error messages, diagnostic codes, memory dumps, or troubleshooting instructions. They can occur due to hardware malfunctions, corrupted system files, software crashes, overheating, or other critical failures. Error screens vary by operating system and device, with some of the most well-known examples being the Blue Screen of Death (BSOD) in Windows, the Sad Mac in classic Macintosh computers, and the Kernel Panic in Unix-based systems like Linux and macOS. Game consoles may also have notable crash screens, such as the PlayStation 2 and the Nintendo Wii."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Bsodwindows10.png/330px-Bsodwindows10.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_crash_screen","wikidata":"Q4530493"},{"title":"Death","blocks":[{"text":"Death is the end of an organism's life. It is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Entropy, nature's tendency toward disorder, can contribute to the breakdown of living systems over time. Upon death, organisms stop exhibiting behavior and generally begin to decompose through various physical and chemical processes. Death can occur at any stage of development, including in infancy. A person may experience clinical death that is reversible, resulting in some cases in a near-death experience. In humans, establishing death of the brain determines that an individual has irreversibly died."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg/330px-Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death","wikidata":"Q4"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/BSoD_in_Windows_1.0.png/500px-BSoD_in_Windows_1.0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/BSoD_in_Windows_1.0.png/500px-BSoD_in_Windows_1.0.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The boot screens of Windows 1.01 and 2.03 when running on an incorrect DOS version, displaying mojibake","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABSoD_in_Windows_1.0.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Windows_NT_3.51_BSOD_ita.png/500px-Windows_NT_3.51_BSOD_ita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Windows_NT_3.51_BSOD_ita.png/500px-Windows_NT_3.51_BSOD_ita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The original blue screen of death first appeared in Windows NT 3.1; this was a later version as it appears on the Italian version of Windows NT 3.51","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindows_NT_3.51_BSOD_ita.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Windows_3.1_BSoD_%28fixed%29.png/500px-Windows_3.1_BSoD_%28fixed%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Windows_3.1_BSoD_%28fixed%29.png/500px-Windows_3.1_BSoD_%28fixed%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The rudimentary task manager screen from Windows 3.1x, often confused with the blue screen of death due to its similarities","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindows_3.1_BSoD_(fixed).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/029-bsod_Windows_2000.png/500px-029-bsod_Windows_2000.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/029-bsod_Windows_2000.png/500px-029-bsod_Windows_2000.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A029-bsod_Windows_2000.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Windows_XP_BSOD.png/500px-Windows_XP_BSOD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Windows_XP_BSOD.png/500px-Windows_XP_BSOD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWindows_XP_BSOD.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/BSOD_-_Windows_XP%2C_Vista%2C_7.png/500px-BSOD_-_Windows_XP%2C_Vista%2C_7.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/BSOD_-_Windows_XP%2C_Vista%2C_7.png/500px-BSOD_-_Windows_XP%2C_Vista%2C_7.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABSOD_-_Windows_XP%2C_Vista%2C_7.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Windows_8_BSOD.png/500px-Windows_8_BSOD.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser